{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly, smaller than n\n    >>> largest_divisor(15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 1:\n        # According to the problem description, divisors smaller than n are sought.\n        # For n=1, there are no divisors smaller than n.\n        # For n <= 1, it's not well-defined or meaningful in this context.\n        # However, the example implies positive integers.\n        # The design document stated \"Input must be an integer greater than 1\".\n        # Let's adhere to the spirit of the problem (finding a divisor *smaller* than n).\n        # A prime example is 1, which has no divisors smaller than 1.\n        # The prompt asks for \"largest number that divides n evenly, smaller than n\".\n        # For n=1, there is no such number.\n        # Based on the design, n > 1 is a valid input.\n        # If the problem statement implies n > 1, then handling n <= 1 could raise an error or return a specific value.\n        # The docstring example is for 15, which is > 1.\n        # Given the existing design, a ValueError seems appropriate for n <= 1.\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return i\n    # This part of the code should ideally not be reached if n > 1,\n    # as 1 is always a divisor for any integer n.\n    # The loop iterates down to 1, so 1 will always be returned if no other divisor is found.\n    # However, for completeness and adhering to the loop logic, we can return 1 here.\n    return 1\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly, smaller than n\n    >>> largest_divisor(15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\n    for i in range(n // 2, 0, -1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return i\n    return 1\n```"}
