{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\ndef monotonic(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return True if list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    An empty list or a list with one element is considered monotonic.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(l) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    is_increasing = True\n    is_decreasing = True\n\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        if l[i] < l[i-1]:\n            is_increasing = False\n        if l[i] > l[i-1]:\n            is_decreasing = False\n\n        # If neither increasing nor decreasing, we can stop early.\n        if not is_increasing and not is_decreasing:\n            return False\n\n    return is_increasing or is_decreasing\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/58", "solution": "```python\ndef common(l1: list, l2: list):\n    \"\"\"Return sorted unique common elements for two lists.\n    >>> common([1, 4, 3, 34, 653, 2, 5], [5, 7, 1, 5, 9, 653, 121])\n    [1, 5, 653]\n    >>> common([5, 3, 2, 8], [3, 2])\n    [2, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Convert lists to sets for efficient common element identification and uniqueness.\n    common_set = set(l1).intersection(set(l2))\n    # Convert the resulting set to a list and sort it in ascending order.\n    return sorted(list(common_set))\n```"}
